  "result": "All three tests pass.\n\nI implemented `netgraph/graph.py` using Approach A, adjacency sets: each node holds a set of neighbours, edges are counted on insertion so `edge_count` is O(1), and `shortest_path` is a breadth-first search that visits neighbours in sorted order and reconstructs the path from a parent map. I chose adjacency lists because the interface is neighbour-centric (`neighbors` and BFS both iterate a node's neighbours), so memory proportional to nodes plus edges and degree-proportional iteration fit better than an n\u00b2 matrix, and edge lookup is still O(1) with sets.",
